  abstract = {Oncology evidence is fragmented across trials, registries, and publications, limiting evidence synthesis and precision oncology. We used large language models (LLMs) to extract and standardize more than 100,000 cancer trials from ClinicalTrials.gov, scored cancer-treatment interactions using clinical outcomes and trial attributes, and trained three collaborative filtering and hybrid recommendation models. The resulting curated database covers 55 cancer types and 12,049 interventions involving 4,443 drugs. The models achieved cross-validated RMSEs of 0.464--0.680 and generated clinically plausible melanoma and non-small cell lung cancer candidate treatment hypotheses, which were reviewed by oncology experts. These results show that combining LLM-based evidence extraction with recommendation modeling can generate treatment hypotheses across cancer types and support oncology evidence synthesis.},
