  abstract = {Biological aging is a complex physiological process beyond what chronological age can capture. Built upon DNA methylation (DNAm) profiles, epigenetic clocks estimate biological age reflective of molecular changes over cellular evolution, which have gained popularity in aging research. Most existing clocks are developed using DNAm data from the Illumina 450K array, which appears unsuitable for studies with DNAm data collected from the higher-resolution 850K EPIC array. A common practice in cross-platform analyses is to restrict calculations to shared CpGs of existing clocks, which discards substantial information and loses age prediction accuracy. To address this gap, we use a neural network architecture to develop an enrichment learning framework that calibrates the existing clock trained on lower-resolution DNAm data to be adaptive to higher-resolution data. We illustrate the proposed method using the childhood and adolescent cohort dataset consisting of 601 samples with ages ranging from 8 to 18 years. The proposed enrichment learning improves the prediction accuracy of epigenetic age compared with the conventional methods that directly apply existing 450K-based clocks. Our neural network modeling framework provides a scalable and generalizable methodology to calibrate existing epigenetic clocks to achieve better adaptiveness for population heterogeneity and advances in DNAm platform technology.},
