  abstract = {Recovering the marginal failure-time distribution is difficult when censoring is informative because the observed data do not identify the relationship between failure time $T$ and censoring time $C$ without additional structure. We pursue marginal $T$ recovery by modeling $(T,C\mid Z)$ with a joint conditional flow. Two monotone flows represent the failure and censoring distributions. Under informative censoring, a one-dimensional shared-latent structure represents their dependence and is integrated out during training. We combine the censored likelihood with a differentiable Soft--Nelson--Aalen Wasserstein regularizer, which compares the empirical survival curve with a curve constructed from a model-generated population. In four independent-censoring simulations with $51\%$--$77\%$ censoring, the joint conditional flow remains comparable to deep $T$-only baselines on marginal $T$ recovery. In the shared-frailty experiment at $\rho=1$, it obtains $T$-KS $0.039$, compared with $0.057$ for the study-specific deep-copula comparison and $0.048$ for the Kaplan--Meier reference line. Across the informative-censoring mechanisms studied, it has lower $T$-KS than the evaluated deep generative baselines; improvement over Kaplan--Meier occurs in the frailty-type settings. The generative formulation enables paired $(T,C)$ sampling and population-level distribution matching; the empirical evaluation focuses on marginal failure-time recovery.},
