  abstract = {Multi-judge LLM benchmarks are costly because each candidate LLM must answer a fixed set of questions and each response is scored by several LLM judges. The standard approach to benchmarking a candidate reveals the scores for all entries of an $N \times J$ matrix corresponding to $N$ questions and $J$ judges. While full-matrix evaluation is natural, many uses of benchmarks reduce to \textit{tier placement}, in which the full-matrix mean is compared to a prespecified cutpoint, e.g., deciding whether a model clears a deployment cutoff. Because tier placement can often be accomplished without obtaining every score in the matrix, we introduce STAVAT, a stratified anytime-valid atom sampling procedure for certifying the same tier that full multi-judge evaluation would assign. STAVAT combines three ingredients that each improve cost efficiency: (i) e-processes for sampling without replacement, (ii) adaptive sampling across stratifications of benchmark questions, and (iii) atom sampling, which defines the basic sampling unit as a single judge--question entry rather than the full judge panel for a single question. Across 74 candidates and five benchmarks, STAVAT reduces judge calls by $13$-$41$\% compared to the standard approach.},
